    },
    "policy.privacy": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Yew Harness runs on your own computer, and Yew Harness on your phone (iPhone or Android) connects only to Yew Harness on your own computer; the Android app also checks for new versions, as described below. Tenon AI does not collect or receive your content in Yew Harness, and Yew Harness contains no analytics, telemetry or reporting code.\n\nData kept on your computer: sessions, messages, text you typed into a message box and have not sent, what Yew says when she observes, tool call records, permissions you granted, letters between sessions, the decisions you make in the approval center, scheduled tasks and settings are stored in a database in Yew Harness's data folder (~/.yew by default); provider API keys, and the access tokens, refresh tokens and client registrations Yew Harness gets when you sign in to an MCP server in your browser, are stored in the system keychain; MCP server settings are kept in mcp.json in the data folder, including keys you enter and the ID and secret of a Google client you created yourself; the standing instructions you write in Settings are stored in AGENTS.md in the data folder, are added to every session and go to the session's model provider with the conversation; the character you write for Yew is stored in persona.md in the data folder; very long command output is stored in the tmp folder of the data folder; every version of the artifacts sessions save (documents, pages, data and charts) is stored in its artifacts folder. Yew Harness's built-in browser uses your own Chrome by default, through the Playwright extension, and its cookies, cache and history stay in your Chrome; if you choose a separate window in Settings, that window's profile (cookies, cache and history of the sites you signed in to there) is stored in the browser folder of the data folder. When you set Yew Harness to start at login with yew service, its service log (start-up, errors and relay connection lines, without conversation content) is stored in the logs folder of the data folder. When you open the interface in the Mac app, macOS keeps for it, under cn.tenonai.apps.yew.desktop in ~/Library, the cookie that signs you in to Yew Harness (it expires after 30 days without use), a cache of the interface's files, a few local interface settings and the window's position and size, without any session content. This data stays until you delete it. If you set a decision log file in Settings, Yew Harness adds each decision to that file. If you set a roles folder in Settings, Yew Harness writes each standing session's role into a file in that folder, along with a README.md that describes the folder, and when the folder is in a git repository, it commits each change to that repository (on your computer only; nothing is pushed).\n\nPhone access: when you turn on phone access on your computer, Yew Harness on the computer connects to a relay, and Yew Harness on your phone talks to it through the relay. The default relay is run by Tenon AI; you can also run your own. The two ends are encrypted end to end: the relay only passes ciphertext along and can neither read nor keep the content. For network security, the relay run by Tenon AI keeps a connection log: the time, the IP address, the path requested (which carries your computer's identifier, a hash of its public key) and the status, without content, deleted automatically after 184 days. The relay server is in mainland China. Yew Harness on the computer records each paired phone: its name, public key, when it was paired and when it last connected. Yew Harness on iPhone keeps the pairing and its own key in the iOS keychain, on that device only. With notifications on, Yew Harness on iPhone gives the push token it got from Apple to your computer over the encrypted channel, and the computer keeps it; the computer encrypts what a notification says for that iPhone and hands it to Apple's push service through the relay. The relay and Apple handle the push token and the ciphertext, and cannot read what the notification says. Yew Harness on Android keeps the pairing and its own key in a private file of the app, encrypted with a key in the Android Keystore, on that device only; it is left out of system backups and device transfers. Yew Harness on Android uses no push service and has no push token: with notifications on, a foreground service keeps a connection to the relay open in the background, and the computer sends what a notification says to the phone over the end-to-end encrypted channel; the relay only passes ciphertext along. Android shows a standing notification for this connection, and notifications do not arrive while the system has stopped Yew Harness. Scanning the pairing QR code uses the camera; the picture is read on the phone and is neither kept nor sent. The phone also remembers whether notifications are on and the ids of the items it has already seen, without their content.\n\nData sent to third parties: the model provider you choose for a session receives the conversation sent to it, including files the session read, command output, what MCP tools return (such as the emails, events and files in a mailbox, calendar or drive you connected), and the page content and screenshots a session obtains when it uses the browser and computer control tools; a screenshot captures whatever is on the screen at that moment, including other apps' windows; with your own Chrome, a session can open sites you are signed in to and read their pages, in the tabs you give it and the tabs it opens, and a script it runs in a page can also read the cookies that site lets scripts read, all of which goes to the provider as well; that provider's privacy policy applies. The default provider, DeepSeek, is a service of the Chinese company DeepSeek; how it handles and stores this content is set out in its privacy policy. If you choose a provider outside mainland China, this content goes abroad. Naming a new session, and reading whether the end of a reply leaves you a question, are done by a model of the provider chosen for the session by default. When you choose a side model in Settings, what naming uses (up to 2,000 characters of your first message and 500 of the start of the reply) and what the reading uses (up to the last 3,000 characters of the reply) go to the provider of that model, and its privacy policy applies; when that provider is outside mainland China, this content goes abroad. When you choose an observer model in Settings, after each turn you start, your last message (up to 2,000 characters), the name and arguments of each call the session made in that turn (up to 30 calls, 200 characters each), the session's reply (up to 4,000 characters) and Yew's character notes go to the provider of that model, which writes what Yew says under the reply; its privacy policy applies, and when that provider is outside mainland China, this content goes abroad. Nothing is sent when no observer model is chosen, or for a session where you turned observing off. When a session fetches a web page, opens one in the built-in browser or has your default browser open an address, that site receives the request; a site you signed in to in those browsers (the built-in browser uses your own Chrome by default) receives requests that carry your sign-in. When a session searches the web, the search service it uses receives only the query: Bocha, Tavily or Brave if you saved a key for one of them, and otherwise DeepSeek, with the DeepSeek key you saved; that service's privacy policy applies, and with a search service outside mainland China (Tavily, Brave), the query goes abroad. By default Yew Harness starts two open-source MCP servers: a browser (Microsoft's Playwright MCP) and computer control (computer-use-mcp). They run on your computer, and you can turn them off in Settings. Yew Harness downloads and installs these two, and the common servers you add with one click that run on your computer, from npm the first time it uses them; npm receives the download request. Yew Harness installed globally with npm asks the registry your npm uses (by default registry.npmjs.org, outside mainland China) once a day for the newest version number of @tenonai/yew, and the Android app asks Gitee once a day for its newest release; the request carries only the version of Yew Harness or of the app, the registry or Gitee sees your IP address, and its privacy policy applies; both checks are on by default and can be turned off under About. When you click Update, npm downloads the new version from the same registry. An MCP server you add (it is written in mcp.json) receives what a session passes to its tools, and a service you signed in to in your browser receives requests made with your account. These services' own privacy policies apply; with a service outside mainland China (such as Google, Linear or Vercel), what is passed to it goes abroad. In the Default permission mode, these tools ask you before each call, until you choose Always allow for a tool; the clicks, typing and key presses of computer control, and the browser's actions on pages when it uses your own Chrome (clicking, typing, opening addresses, running scripts and the like), cannot be set to Always allow and ask every time. In the Auto mode, what would be asked goes first to a separate check by the model: when it finds that you asked for the step, the step runs, and otherwise you are asked; the check sends what you wrote in that session, the session's role and the step to the provider chosen for the session, without web pages, files or what tools returned. MCP tools that Yew Harness recognizes as sending, publishing or deleting something, and operations that cannot be taken back, such as publishing, sending data out, commands on remote machines and deleting files outside the workspace, are not given to the check in the Auto mode: you are asked directly. In the Skip mode, you are not asked about anything, these operations included, and risky calls are marked in the record; computer control is not asked about either, and it can click anything on the screen, including the buttons in Yew Harness that wait for your answer. Commands you set in hooks.json in the data folder run when a session starts, when you send a message, before and after a tool call and when a session stops, and receive what is at hand then (what you wrote, and a tool's name, arguments and result); what they do with it is up to those commands. Their output when a session starts and when you send a message is added to the conversation and goes to the model provider like the rest of it.\n\nAccess, export and deletion: you can look at the files in the data folder directly. To delete a provider's key, delete it in Settings or run yew key delete; to remove a paired phone, remove it under Phone access on the computer, or unpair on the phone; to sign out of an MCP server, choose Sign out under MCP servers: where the service supports it, Yew Harness first asks it to cancel the tokens, then deletes the ones kept on this computer; to make sure the access you granted is revoked, check or remove it in that service's account settings; to disconnect the built-in browser from your Chrome, click the Playwright extension's icon in Chrome and disconnect it, or remove the extension; to clear the separate window's sign-ins, delete the browser folder in the data folder; to clear what the Mac app keeps, quit it and delete the items starting with cn.tenonai.apps.yew.desktop in the WebKit, Caches, HTTPStorages and Preferences folders of ~/Library; to stop writing the roles folder, clear its path in Settings: Yew Harness writes nothing more there, and the files already written and the commits in the repository are yours to delete; to delete something Yew said, remove it where it is shown; to delete everything, stop yew serve and delete the data folder and, if you used the Mac app, those items too, and the files in the roles folder if you set one.\n\nContact: support@tenonai.cn."
          }
        },
        "zh-Hans": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Yew Harness 是在你自己的电脑上运行的软件，手机上的 Yew Harness（iPhone 和安卓）只连接你自己电脑上的 Yew Harness；安卓应用另外会查有没有新版本，见下文。榫卯 Tenon AI 不收集、不接收你在 Yew Harness 里的内容，Yew Harness 里没有统计、遥测或上报代码。\n\n保存在你电脑上的数据：会话、消息、输入框里还没发出去的文字、Yew 旁观时说的话、工具调用记录、操作授权、会话之间的信件、你在审批中心做的决定、定时任务和设置存在 Yew Harness 的数据目录（默认 ~/.yew）里的数据库中；服务商的 API Key，以及你在浏览器里登录 MCP 服务得到的访问令牌、刷新令牌和 Yew Harness 为它注册的客户端，存在系统钥匙串里；MCP 服务的配置写在数据目录的 mcp.json 里，包括你填的密钥和你自己建的 Google 客户端的 ID 和密钥；你在设置里写的固定说明存在数据目录的 AGENTS.md 里，会放进每个会话，随对话发给会话所选的模型服务商；你给 Yew 写的人设存在数据目录的 persona.md 里；超长的命令输出存在数据目录的 tmp 文件夹里；会话存下的产物（文档、页面、数据和图表）的各个版本存在数据目录的 artifacts 文件夹里。Yew Harness 自带的浏览器默认经 Playwright 扩展使用你自己的 Chrome，Cookie、缓存和浏览记录留在你的 Chrome 里；在设置里改用单独的窗口时，那个窗口的资料（你在里面登录过的网站的 Cookie、缓存和浏览记录）存在数据目录的 browser 文件夹里。用 yew service 设了开机启动时，运行日志（启动、出错和连接中继的记录，不含对话内容）存在数据目录的 logs 文件夹里。用 Mac 应用打开界面时，macOS 替这个应用把登录 Yew Harness 用的 Cookie（30 天不用就失效）、界面文件的缓存、界面的几项本地设置和窗口的位置大小，存在 ~/Library 里 cn.tenonai.apps.yew.desktop 名下，不含会话内容。这些数据一直保存，直到你删除。你在设置里指定了审批记录文件时，Yew Harness 把每个决定追加到那个文件里。你在设置里指定了职责文件夹时，Yew Harness 把每个常设会话的职责写成那个文件夹里的一个文件，另写一份说明这个文件夹的 README.md；文件夹在 git 仓库里时，每次改动提交到那个仓库（只在你的电脑上，不推送）。\n\n手机访问：你在电脑上打开「允许手机访问」后，电脑上的 Yew Harness 连接中继服务，手机上的 Yew Harness 经中继和它通信。默认的中继由榫卯 Tenon AI 运行，你也可以自己部署。两端之间端到端加密，中继只转发密文，看不到也不保存内容。榫卯 Tenon AI 运行的中继为保障网络安全记录连接日志：时间、IP 地址、请求的路径（其中有你电脑的标识，是它公钥的哈希）和状态，不含内容，保存 184 天后自动删除。中继服务器位于中国境内。电脑上的 Yew Harness 记下配对的手机：名称、公钥、配对时间和最近连接的时间。iPhone 上的 Yew Harness 把配对信息和自己的密钥存在 iOS 钥匙串里，只存在这台设备上。打开通知后，iPhone 把 Apple 给它的推送令牌经加密通道交给你的电脑保存；电脑把通知的内容为这台 iPhone 加密，经中继交给 Apple 的推送服务。中继和 Apple 经手推送令牌和密文，看不到通知的内容。安卓上的 Yew Harness 把配对信息和自己的密钥存在应用的私有文件里，用 Android 密钥库里的密钥加密，只存在这台设备上，不进系统备份和换机传输。安卓上的 Yew Harness 不使用推送服务，也没有推送令牌：打开通知后，它用一个前台服务在后台保持到中继的连接，电脑经端到端加密的通道把通知的内容发给手机，中继只转发密文；系统会为这条连接显示一条常驻通知，系统停掉 Yew Harness 时收不到通知。扫描配对二维码时用到相机，画面只在手机上识别，不保存也不发出。手机上另记着通知的开关和已经看到的待办的编号，不含内容。\n\n发给第三方的数据：你为会话选的模型服务商会收到发给它的对话内容，包括会话读到的文件、命令输出、MCP 工具返回的内容（比如你连接的邮箱、日历和云端硬盘里的邮件、日程和文件），以及会话使用浏览器和电脑操控工具时取得的网页内容和屏幕截图；屏幕截图会拍下当时屏幕上显示的内容，包括其他应用的窗口；用你自己的 Chrome 时，会话能在你选给它的和它自己开的标签页里打开你已登录的网站、读取页面内容，在页面里运行脚本时还能读到这个网站允许脚本读取的 Cookie，这些同样会发给服务商；这部分适用该服务商的隐私政策。默认服务商 DeepSeek 由中国公司深度求索提供，内容怎样处理和存放以它的隐私政策为准；你选境外服务商时，这些内容会发往境外。给新会话起名、读一条回复的末尾是不是给你留了问题，这两件事默认由会话所选服务商的模型来做。你在设置里另选了旁路模型时，起名用到的会话开头（你的第一条消息最多 2000 个字、回复开头最多 500 个字）和读回复用到的回复末尾（最多 3000 个字）会发给那个模型的服务商，适用它的隐私政策；它在境外时，这些内容会发往境外。你在设置里选了旁观模型时，你发起的每一轮结束后，你的上一条消息（最多 2000 个字）、会话这一轮每个操作的名称和参数（最多 30 个，每个最多 200 个字）、会话的回复（最多 4000 个字）和 Yew 的人设会发给那个模型的服务商，由它写出 Yew 在回复下面说的话，适用它的隐私政策；它在境外时，这些内容会发往境外。不选旁观模型，或在会话设置里对某个会话关掉时，不发。会话抓取网页、用自带的浏览器打开网页，或让你的默认浏览器打开网址时，网站会收到请求；你在这些浏览器里登录过的网站（自带的浏览器默认用的就是你自己的 Chrome），会收到带着你登录状态的请求。会话联网搜索时，所用的搜索服务只会收到查询词：你存了博查、Tavily 或 Brave 的密钥时用它们，否则用你存的 DeepSeek 密钥，由 DeepSeek 搜索；适用该搜索服务的隐私政策；用境外的搜索服务（Tavily、Brave）时，查询词会发往境外。Yew Harness 默认启动两个开源的 MCP 服务：浏览器（微软的 Playwright MCP）和电脑操控（computer-use-mcp），它们在你的电脑上运行，可以在设置里停用。这两个服务，以及你一键添加的、要在本机运行的常用服务，Yew Harness 第一次使用时从 npm 下载安装，npm 会收到下载请求。用 npm 全局安装的 Yew Harness 默认每天向你的 npm 所用的源（默认是境外的 registry.npmjs.org）查询一次 @tenonai/yew 的最新版本号，安卓应用默认每天向 Gitee 查询一次最新发布；请求只带 Yew Harness 或这个应用的版本号，对方会看到你的 IP 地址，适用它们的隐私政策；两处都可以在「关于」里关掉。你点「更新」时，npm 从同一个源下载新版本。你添加的 MCP 服务（写在 mcp.json 里）会收到会话调用它的工具时传的内容；你在浏览器里登录过的服务，会收到以你的账号发出的请求。这些服务适用各自的隐私政策；选境外的服务（比如 Google、Linear、Vercel）时，传给它的内容会发往境外。在默认的授权方式下，这些工具每次调用都先问你，你对某个工具选了「总是允许」后不再问；电脑操控的点击、输入和按键，以及用你自己的 Chrome 时浏览器在页面上的操作（点击、输入、打开网址、运行脚本等），不能选「总是允许」，每次都问。授权方式选「自动」时，本来要问你的先交给一次单独的模型检查，它认定是你要做的就直接执行，否则再问你；这次检查会把你在这个会话里写的话、会话的职责和这一步要做的事发给这个会话所选的服务商，不含网页、文件和工具返回的内容。Yew Harness 认出会发送、发布或删除东西的 MCP 工具，以及发布、往外发送数据、远程主机上的命令、删除工作区外的文件这类做错了收不回的操作，选「自动」时不交给检查，直接问你。选「跳过」时什么都不问，这些操作也直接执行，只在记录里标出有风险的调用；电脑操控也不问，它能点到屏幕上的任何东西，包括 Yew Harness 里等你回答的按钮。你在数据目录的 hooks.json 里配的命令，会在会话开始、你发消息、调用工具前后和会话停下时运行，收到当时的内容（你写的话，工具的名字、参数和结果），拿这些做什么由这些命令决定；会话开始和你发消息时它们的输出会加进对话，和其他对话内容一样发给模型服务商。\n\n查询、导出与删除：你可以直接查看数据目录里的文件。删除某个服务商的 Key：在设置里删除，或运行 yew key delete；删除配对的手机：在电脑上的「手机访问」里移除，或在手机上断开配对；退出 MCP 服务的登录：在「MCP 服务」里点「退出登录」，服务支持时 Yew Harness 先请它作废令牌，再删除本机保存的令牌；要确认授权已经撤销，到该服务的账号设置里查看或移除；断开自带的浏览器和你的 Chrome：在 Chrome 里点 Playwright 扩展的图标断开连接，或移除这个扩展；删除单独窗口里的登录状态：删除数据目录的 browser 文件夹；删除 Mac 应用存的登录状态和缓存：退出应用后，删除 ~/Library 的 WebKit、Caches、HTTPStorages 和 Preferences 文件夹里以 cn.tenonai.apps.yew.desktop 开头的项；不再写职责文件夹：在设置里清空路径，Yew Harness 之后不再写入；已经写进去的文件和仓库里的提交，由你自己删除；删除 Yew 说过的话：在那句话上点删除；删除全部数据：停掉 yew serve 后删除数据目录，用过 Mac 应用的再删除上面这些项，设过职责文件夹的再删除那里的文件。\n\n联系我们：support@tenonai.cn。"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "policy.terms": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Yew Harness is open source under the MIT License; see the LICENSE file in the code repository. Under that license you may use, copy, modify and distribute Yew Harness. Yew Harness is provided as is, without warranty of any kind, and to the extent the law allows we are not liable for losses arising from its use.\n\nYew Harness reads and writes files, runs commands and goes online as you allow. Choose a suitable workspace, permission mode and permission rules for each session. In Default, deleting files, pushing and similar operations always ask you first; in Auto, a check by the model first decides whether you asked for such an operation, and the check can be wrong; operations that cannot be taken back, such as publishing, sending data out, commands on remote machines and deleting files outside the workspace, are not given to the check: you are asked directly. In Skip, you are asked about nothing except raising a spending limit: these operations and commands that run outside the system sandbox run too. In Skip, a session asks you first only about what you required in its role and the project's instructions; it may miss this or not follow it, and Yew Harness no longer stops it. In Skip, computer control is not asked about either and can click anything on the screen, including the buttons in Yew Harness that wait for your answer; when you use Skip, turn computer control off or keep an eye on what it does. The browser and computer control tools, and MCP servers you signed in to, can act with your sign-ins in web pages, other apps and those services, including sending, submitting, buying and deleting; think before you choose Auto or Skip, or Always allow for these tools, and keep an eye on what the session is doing. The model provider you choose and the MCP servers you connect apply their own terms.\n\nTenon AI provides the default relay free of charge, only for Yew Harness's phone access. It is provided as is, and we may change or stop it; connections that abuse it may be limited or closed. You can also run your own relay.\n\nThe Yew Harness, Yew and Tenon AI names and logos, and the design and images of the character Yew, are not licensed under the MIT License."
          }
        },
        "zh-Hans": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "Yew Harness 以 MIT 许可证开源，许可证全文见代码仓库的 LICENSE 文件。按许可证，你可以使用、复制、修改和分发 Yew Harness；Yew Harness 按现状提供，不附带任何担保，在法律允许的范围内，我们不对使用 Yew Harness 造成的损失承担责任。\n\nYew Harness 会按你的授权读写文件、执行命令和访问网络。请为会话选择合适的工作区、授权方式和权限规则。默认方式下，删除文件、推送这类操作每次都先问你；选「自动」时，这类操作先由模型检查是不是你要它做的，检查可能判断错，发布、往外发送数据、远程主机上的命令、删除工作区外的文件这类做错了收不回的操作不交给检查，直接问你；选「跳过」时除了提高花费上限，什么都不问，这些收不回的操作和在系统沙箱外运行的命令也直接执行。这时只有你在会话职责和项目说明里写明要先问的事，会话才会自己来问你；会话可能漏问或不照做，Yew Harness 不再替你拦。跳过方式下电脑操控也不问，它能点到屏幕上的任何东西，包括 Yew Harness 里等你回答的按钮；用跳过方式时请停用电脑操控，或留意它在做什么。浏览器、电脑操控工具和你登录过的 MCP 服务能以你已登录的身份在网页、其他应用和这些服务里操作，包括发送、提交、购买和删除；选「自动」「跳过」，或对这类工具选「总是允许」前请想清楚，并留意会话正在做什么。你选的模型服务商和你连接的 MCP 服务适用它们自己的条款。\n\n默认的中继由榫卯 Tenon AI 免费提供，只用于 Yew Harness 的手机访问，按现状提供，我们可能调整或停止；滥用的连接会被限制或断开。你也可以自己部署中继。\n\nYew Harness、Yew 和榫卯 Tenon AI 的名称和标志，以及角色 Yew 的形象和图片，不在 MIT 许可证的授权范围内。"
          }
        }
      }
    },
